<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: The Rapist Says He's Sorry
</h4>

<p>
Mr. Heckert, I very much value the work you put into this.  One of my cousins, kind of a big brother to me, grew up to be a serial rapist-murderer, and was legally executed for this a few years ago.  I myself went the other way:  good education, good career, raising my kids as well as I can, going to church.  I spent the night of my cousin's execution at my church, praying for him &amp; his victims.  In my extended family of 100+ people, we've had 10 killings in all:  8 murders, 1 suicide in jail, 1 self-defense killing.  Sometimes, my kin is the victim, sometimes they're the perp. <br />
<br />
Where I live and work, the people I hang around with are very remote from all this.  So overall, I spend a _lot_ of time thinking about how far or near I am from people like Gaff, my cousin, and my troubled family.  It eats at me, really.<br />
<br />
Up until about halfway through my cousin's many appeals, I was against the death penalty -  not because of him, but just because I was following the usual progressive rationale on the subject.  But one night, I Googled my cousin's name, and I found court documents about other capital murder cases that cited one of my cousin's failed appeals.  Those documents inadvertently changed my mind about the death penalty, despite my love for my cousin, because the documents necessarily laid out in some detail the extreme and wanton brutality of these other defendants' crimes.  Just as the summary word "rape" doesn't convey the horror of what Gaff did, "murder/rape" doesn't do justice to my cousin's victims' suffering.  And to my surprise, the other cases' appellate documents showed to me that my cousin's cruelty wasn't particularly unique;  the capital murder cases I read about all had that extreme cruelty in common, though each murderer's preferences and details were different.  Trust me, as bad as Gaff's acts were, my cousin (and the others I read about) went much farther down that dark road than Gaff did.<br />
<br />
In sum, I would have little regret about Gaff staying under state control.  It would be nice if the Constitution could cover and repair every weird angle and curlicue that people have to offer, but that's too much to ask, and meanwhile people like Gaff and my cousin are running around loose.<br />
<br />
I'm sorry to remain anonymous, but I don't want to embarrass my parents and my many aunts, uncles, and cousins, who all have tried put this stuff behind them.
</p>
