                            <a name="comment6445"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: The Rapist Says He's Sorry
</h4>

<p>
Jones, your praise and sympathy are very kind;  thank you.  I've occasionally thought of trying to write all this down, but besides my family's wish for privacy, I don't know how I could write it.  It seems to me that there are three ways to present all this sorrow:<br />
   * a dark, terrifying novel, as you suggest;<br />
   * a personal memoir;<br />
   * a dry &amp; intense journalistic account.<br />
<br />
Novel:  I'm not much capable of the lyrical writing that a novel would sometimes call for.  My native bluntness enables me well to write a brief "punch-the-reader-in-the-nose" account, but a novel calls for more writerly scope than I can bring to the task.  But sometimes I fantasize about trying to do this, anyway.<br />
<br />
Memoir:  Perhaps because of my own personal losses (not the killings, but other losses), I kind of disapprove of memoirs like Angela's Ashes and Jerzy Koszinski's books.  They seem to me whiny.  If instead I wrote a Horatio-Alger-type account of how far I came from my origins, I'd be degrading my family's losses, by reducing them to props.<br />
<br />
Journalistic:  No way;  my family has had way too much press attention, and they suffered terribly from the shame of it.<br />
<br />
So, I don't know how to begin.  My wife writes, and we have friends who write, but I can't take such insane material to a writer's group;  people wouldn't come back after the coffee break.  As for getting writing advice from my wife - well, she got tired of my tracking blood onto the rug, a long time ago.<br />
<br />
BTW, I'm sorry to have been careless, but I got the number of killings wrong.  The correct number is 12, not 10.  Sometimes I lose track.  When I wrote last night's comment, I forgot to count my cousin's own execution by the state, and the murder of his nephew, 10 years earlier.  He was such a fine kid.  This crap makes my head want to explode.
</p>
